[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the constraint that most often turns a "notifications system" into one that silently never fires.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a notification can reach you when you're not looking at the app. For a local, no-backend app that's
  a hard technical boundary, not a preference.

  - [1mIn-app only[0m — a bell/badge and a feed inside the page. Always works, zero permissions, fully testable. But a due
    date that passes while the tab is closed is only discovered when you next open the app. Honestly: this is a [3mtask [0m
    [3mlist with an unread indicator[0m, and that may be exactly right.
  - [1mOS notifications while open[0m — the Web Notifications API, fired from a page timer. Real toasts. Requires a permission
    prompt, and still only fires while the tab is alive. Browsers also throttle timers in background tabs, so "9:00am
    sharp" becomes "sometime after you next focus it."
  - [1mOS notifications while closed[0m — needs a service worker plus the Push API, and push needs a server to send from. That
    contradicts the no-backend decision we just made; it's the piece that forces the architecture to grow.

  The thing worth naming: the strongest version of "notify me" is the one we specifically ruled out by staying local.
  I'd rather you choose that tradeoff than discover it when a reminder doesn't arrive.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-app only, with a catch-up sweep on load[0m — on open, compute everything that became due or changed
  since your last visit and show it as unread. It degrades honestly (nothing pretends to be real-time), it needs no
  permission, and it's the same event-log query the OS-notification path would use later, so upgrading is additive
  rather than a rewrite.
